  "notes": "Pop/R&B-forward country — \"the most genre-agnostic radio catalogue of the late 2010s, with funk, disco and tropical-house cuts sitting beside acoustic ballads.\" The key set is a documented oddity: \"D♭ major appearing on an unusual number of his singles alongside D/G/A♭/E♭.\" Drums are programmed-first — \"clap/snap backbeat, 16th hat loops, sampled kicks, and disco-adjacent four-on-the-floor on the dance cuts, with live cymbals and percussion overdubbed. Half-time bridges and beat drop-outs used as structure\" — so the kick carries a four-on-the-floor anchor set and `fourOnFloorInChorus` is authored alongside a high drop-out figure. The bass is the reason no 808 block appears here: the entry calls it \"the most active bass writing in the modern half of this section\" — \"a funk-articulated electric bass (slides, ghost notes, syncopated 16ths)\" over a synth sub — so the live bass is kept, `glideProb` is the highest in this section and `followRootsProb` the lowest. Harmony is \"I–V–vi–IV and ii–V–I pop turnarounds; the funk cuts use a static i7 vamp with a chromatic passing chord,\" which is a raised seventh share and a `vamp` weight rather than a one-chord family. Melody is \"high tenor, syncopated, R&B-inflected with pentatonic runs and falsetto tags — the most ornamented male melodic writing in the modern cohort.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high); the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF] and songdata's 130–175 cluster folds onto the stated spine. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the perc density, the four-on-the-floor share and the counter ratio.",
